course: dictionary
lesson: 2682
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Dick can be a name, and it is an old short form of Richard. «Dick Whittington» is a story about a boy who becomes Lord Mayor of London. Read the words around the name to find out which meaning is meant.
explain: Context tells you which meaning to use. In «Moby-Dick», Dick is part of the whale's name, so it is not a word for a person. Always ask: does my meaning fit the sentence? Try it in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Some slang words are rude, and Dick is sometimes used that way. That is not a word for school, so do not repeat it. If you hear it, tell a grown-up. When you read stories, stick to the meanings that fit the sentence.
fact: Dick is an old short form of the name Richard.
fact: «Dick Whittington» is a famous story character who becomes Lord Mayor of London with his cat.
fact: «Moby-Dick» is a famous novel about a great white whale.
fact: Some words have a name meaning and a slang meaning; choose the meaning that fits the sentence.
fact: Some slang is rude and is not for school use; tell a grown-up if you hear it.
exercise: In «Dick Whittington», Dick is a name. What kind of word is Dick here? | A type of bird | *A name | A colour | A place
exercise: In the novel «Moby-Dick», what is Dick part of? | *The whale's name | A school subject | The name of a town in London | A type of cat
exercise: Dick is a short form of which name? | Robert | Daniel | Simon | *Richard
test: In the story «Dick Whittington», what does Dick become? | A king | *Lord Mayor of London | A cat | A sailor
test: What is the best way to check a meaning? | Look only at the first letter | Skip the whole text | Guess and never check | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense
exam: Ayo reads «Moby-Dick» and says Dick must mean a person. What is the best answer? | Dick means a person in every story | Dick must be a colour | *Dick is part of the whale's name, so the context shows the meaning | Ayo should stop reading
